Path MTU Discovery as quic-go does it, and the packet size that comes out of it (hysteria client).

quic-go keeps three numbers apart. It sends InitialPacketSize = 1280 until discovery has something
larger (vendor/quic-go/internal/protocol/params.go:5-6, vendor/quic-go/connection.go:2900-2916);
the largest it will ever send is min(MaxPacketBufferSize = 1452, the peer's advertised
max_udp_payload_size) (internal/protocol/protocol.go:107-111, quic-go/connection.go:2474-2483); and between
them it searches. The search halves the interval between the size known to work and the size not
ruled out, sends one probe packet every five smoothed RTTs, keeps three failures before it lowers
the ceiling, and stops when twenty bytes separate the two
(vendor/quic-go/mtu_discoverer.go:14-23, :125-177, :186-241). The probe is a PING padded to size,
sent on its own; a lost probe is reported by loss detection like any other packet, and there is no
timer on it (quic-go/connection.go:2546-2563).

Upstream ngtcp2 does none of that the same way:

  - a path starts at NGTCP2_MAX_UDP_PAYLOAD_SIZE, the 1200 every path must carry, where quic-go's
    client starts at 1280;
  - the padding of a datagram goes to that same constant 1200 rather than to the size the path is
    known to take, so PMTUD raising the path would not raise what the client pads to;
  - discovery walks a fixed table of four sizes — 1406, 1342, 1232, 1444 — each tried up to three
    times against a PTO timer, and it stops at whatever of them the path accepted;
  - the ceiling it probes towards is the same setting as the padding target, so the two cannot be
    chosen separately.

This client therefore had discovery switched off and its ceiling pinned at 1280: with the numbers
tied together the only probe ngtcp2 would have sent was 1232 bytes — below the 1280 we already send
successfully, at a fixed place in every connection, and nothing the reference can produce.

This patch unties them and replaces the search. lib/ngtcp2_pmtud.c is quic-go's mtuFinder:
max_udp_payload_size is its min, fail_udp_payload_sizes its lost array, and probelen the midpoint.
NGTCP2_INITIAL_UDP_PAYLOAD_SIZE (1280) is where a client's path starts, conn_dgram_padding pads to
the size the path is known to take, the probe timer is gone — a lost probe reaches the search
through rtb_on_pkt_lost, as it reaches quic-go's through the frame handler of its PING — and
conn_start_pmtud begins the clock at the instant discovery starts, so the first probe is one delay
later rather than at once.

Measured through a quic-go server's own qlog, against the same server minutes apart: the reference
client's largest packet is 1441 bytes and ours was 100 — it never probed, and every datagram it sent
for the life of the connection was 1280 where the reference's were 1366 and up. With this patch the
first probe is 1366 bytes, which is the number the reference sends.

The probe table in the settings (:member:`ngtcp2_settings.pmtud_probes`) is no longer read; the
fields stay because lib/ngtcp2_settings.c computes the size of a NGTCP2_SETTINGS_V2 structure from
the offset of the second one. Both are documented as unused in the public header.

Applies on top of 0007, whose assertion on the length of a written datagram this patch also corrects:
a probe packet is deliberately larger than the size the path is known to take, so the length to
measure it against is the caller's buffer, not the shaped datagram.

